---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import timeline from "../../../docs/screenshots/timeline.png";
import record from "../../../docs/screenshots/record.png";
import places from "../../../docs/screenshots/places.png";
import journeys from "../../../docs/screenshots/journeys.png";
import trackDetail from "../../../docs/screenshots/track-detail.png";
---

<Base
  title="Breadcrumb"
  description="Breadcrumb is an Android app that keeps a timeline of where you have been: trips, the stays between them and the places they happened at, recorded on the phone and kept there."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <h1>A timeline of where you have been.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">
        Breadcrumb is an Android app that keeps one. Each day holds the trips made, the stays
        between them and the places they happened at. Trips are recorded on their own, from the
        phone's detected activity, and everything recorded stays on the phone: there is no account
        and no server.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-meta">Open source · Android 8 and up</p>
      <p class="hero-meta"><a href="#install">In closed testing on Google Play</a></p>
    </div>

    <Image
      class="hero-shot"
      src={timeline}
      width={340}
      densities={[1, 2]}
      priority
      alt="Timeline of trips and stays, grouped by day"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <Image src={record} width={280} densities={[1, 2]} alt="Record tab with this month's totals" />
    <div>
      <h2>How trips are recorded</h2>
      <p>
        With Auto recording on, Android's activity recognition tells the app when you start walking,
        running, cycling or driving. A trip is recorded from then until you are still again, and
        labelled with how you moved. Motion the phone cannot name is recorded as <em>Moving</em>; the
        label on any trip can be changed afterwards, including to taxi, boat, public transit or
        flight, which recognition never reports on its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        GPS runs only while a trip is being recorded. A recognised departure that never produces a
        position switches it off again. A journey sat still through, a train or a taxi, can escape
        recognition, so the app also notices you have left where you stopped by other means, each
        of them a setting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature flip">
    <Image src={places} width={280} densities={[1, 2]} alt="Places tab, named places sorted by visits" />
    <div>
      <h2>Stays and places</h2>
      <p>
        A stay is the time between one trip's end and the next one's start. Stays that keep landing
        in the same spot form a place, which you can name, give a category and a capture radius. Once
        named, the place labels every stay at it, past and future. The Places tab lists them with
        their visit history and shows them on a map.
      </p>
      <p>
        Categories are a fixed set: home, work, shopping and so on. The ones the app suggests for an
        untagged place are learned from the places already tagged.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <Image src={journeys} width={280} densities={[1, 2]} alt="Insights: journeys away from home" />
    <div>
      <h2>Journeys and statistics</h2>
      <p>
        A run of nights spent away from a place tagged Home is a journey. It is named after the
        places where most of its time went, and Insights counts journeys, nights, cities and
        countries per year. City and country names come from a list bundled in the app. While away,
        times are shown on the clock of the place they happened in rather than the phone's.
      </p>
      <p>Statistics show one month's movement and places against the year behind it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature flip">
    <Image src={trackDetail} width={280} densities={[1, 2]} alt="Trip detail with its track colored by elevation" />
    <div>
      <h2>A trip's track</h2>
      <p>
        Each trip's path is drawn on a map, coloured per point by speed, elevation, accuracy,
        satellite count or signal strength. The points the filter rejected, the stops detected along
        the way and the named places at each end are drawn alongside.
      </p>
      <p>
        A trip nothing recorded can be entered by hand from two pins and two times, from the gap in
        the timeline that shows it. Trips a short stop split can be merged; one that should have been
        two can be split. Both can be undone.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="yours">
    <h2>Where the data goes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Everything is stored on the phone. There is no account, no sync, no analytics and no ads.</li>
      <li>
        The network carries map tiles, and one optional thing: a place search used when entering a
        trip by hand, which sends the words typed. It can be switched off in settings.
      </li>
      <li>The whole history can be backed up to a single file and restored from it.</li>
      <li>Trips can be imported from and exported to GPX, one at a time, by day, or all at once.</li>
      <li>A deleted trip waits in Recently deleted for two weeks before it is gone.</li>
      <li>
        An optional app lock asks for a fingerprint or the phone's PIN on opening, and can hide the
        app from screenshots and the recents switcher. Recording is not affected by the lock.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="viewer">
    <h2>The viewer</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/viewer/">viewer</a> is a page on this site that opens a Breadcrumb backup file
      in the browser: every trip on one map, with the same timeline of stays and places the app
      derives. The file is read and kept in the browser only; nothing is uploaded, and the only
      network use is the map.
    </p>
    <p>A backup is exported from the app under Settings → Back up everything.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="get" id="install">
    <h2>Installing</h2>
    <p>
      Breadcrumb is on Google Play in closed testing. Testers are admitted through a Google Group,
      so all three steps are needed, with the same Google account the phone uses.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="https://groups.google.com/g/breadcrumbtesting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Join the testers group</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://play.google.com/apps/testing/io.github.valeronm.breadcrumb" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Opt in to the test</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=io.github.valeronm.breadcrumb" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Install from Google Play</a></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">
      Opting in can take a while to take effect after joining the group. If the store page does not
      offer the install yet, try again later.
    </p>
    <h3>What it needs</h3>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>
        Location set to “Allow all the time”: recording happens with the app closed, and Android
        shows a persistent notification while it does.
      </li>
      <li>
        Physical activity, notifications and an exemption from battery optimisation, each asked for
        when Auto recording is turned on and not before.
      </li>
      <li>Google Play Services, for activity recognition. This is why there is no F-Droid build.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
